      "q": "7. There were a couple of narrow escapes.\nII. The word \u2018intercom\u2019 is used in the text.\nIt is called a merged word as it is made by combining the parts of two words\u2014 \u2018internal\u2019 and \u2018communication\u2019. Merged words are made by combining two words. Combine the words in Column 1 with the words in Column 2 to make merged words. Write the merged word and its meaning.\nOne example has been done for you.\nColumn 1\nColumn 2\nMerged word\nMeaning\nbreakfast\nlunch\n(i) brunch\nmeal eaten between breakfast and lunch hours\nmotor\nhotel\n(ii)\nsmoke\nfog\n(iii)\nsituation\ncomedy\n(iv)\nvideo\nlog\n(v)\nspoon\nfork\n(vi)\nweb\nseminar\n(vii)",
      "a": "wer:\nMerged Word\nRephrased Meaning\n(i) brunch\nA meal eaten between breakfast and lunch.\n(ii) motel\nA roadside hotel meant for travellers and motorists.\n(iii) smog\nA thick fog formed by a mix of smoke and pollutants in the air.\n(iv) sitcom\nA TV show based on funny, everyday situations.\n(v) vlog\nA video-based diary or log shared online.\n(vi) spork\nA utensil that combines features of a spoon and a fork.\n(vii) webinar\nAn online seminar or presentation for a remote audience.\nIII. Read the following sentence from the text and fill in the blank.\nIt will mean that you\u2019re being doubly safeguarded.\nIn the given sentence, \u2018will\u2019 denotes _________ (past/present/future) time.\nAnswer:\nTense / Form\nUsage (Rephrased)\nExamples (Rephrased)\n1. \u2018Will\u2019 (also used for offers, promises, and orders; shows willingness, certainty, or obligation)\nUsed when giving information about future events or talking about events that may happen later.\nI think Kailash House will win the competition."
